{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791801289,"nonce":"Z4Nurui6i7ilmhWDHbYmd9UZ9TA=","title":"Consume pending application wakes in the native libuv bridge","message":"Calling native.window.Window.wake() immediately after constructing a NativeWindowHost can permanently starve native event pumping. The libuv check callback sees external_pending(), notifies JS, and returns without calling pump_external(). Window.poll() drains window events but deliberately leaves the application work flag set; only pump_external() or a blocking window wait consumes that flag. JS callbacks then repeat while the platform gets no further event passes and the initial surface remains unavailable.\n\nReproduced on macOS/Metal, Node 24.2.0, published SDK 0472518 with xwindow 074097c: over five seconds the instrumented bridge recorded 351237 checks/notifications and zero pump calls; the host recorded zero frames, 340603 surface misses, and native occlusionState=8192 (visible bit clear). A direct blocking window wait consumed the wake and a frame arrived about 120ms later. Adding Window.wake() just after open() in tests/native-idle.mjs also fails the unchanged 15-second watchdog with the ordinary production addon.\n\nScope: always route pending work through pump_external(), which consumes application wakes and returns immediately for queued work; preserve event-driven idle blocking and I/O wake behavior. Add the startup wake regression, verify it fails before and passes after the fix, and run native window and full-suite checks plus Linux X11/Wayland CI.\n\nRelated to 748f2d7, but this is not yet a proven explanation for its spontaneous 60-second stalls. Those failures had about 123 surface misses in a minute and no explicit Window.wake trigger has been found in their SDK paths. Keep that investigation open.","files":null}]}